                url: "https://www.9marks.org/article/waters-that-unite-five-truths-about-water-baptism/",
                text: "Con demasiada frecuencia el bautismo se ve como aguas que dividen. En el Nuevo Testamento, sin embargo, el bautismo identifica públicamente a los cristianos con su Señor y entre sí. Sobre todo en Pablo, el bautismo se invoca como un medio de unidad en la iglesia. Quienes han muerto y resucitado con Cristo se reconocen por su bautismo común (Romanos 6:3–6). Como dice Pablo en Gálatas 3:25–29, todos los que son “uno en Cristo Jesús” han sido “bautizados en Cristo”. Por lo tanto, el bautismo es un medio para identificar a quienes son uno en Cristo.",
                original:
                  "Too often baptism is seen as waters that divide. In the New Testament, however, baptism publicly identifies Christians with their Lord and one another. Especially in Paul, baptism is appealed to as a means of unity in the church. Those who have died and risen again with Christ are known by their common baptism (Romans 6:3–6). As Paul says in Galatians 3:25–29, all those who are “one in Christ Jesus” have been “baptized into Christ.” Baptism, therefore, is a means of identifying those who are one in Christ.",
