                  "That baptism has to do with conversion is confirmed by 1 Corinthians 6, 11, Ephesians 5, 26, and Titus 3, 5. In 1 Corinthians 6, 11, Paul says, you were washed in the name of the Lord Jesus Christ and by the Spirit of our God. In Ephesians 5.26, he says Christ died to sanctify the church, having cleansed her by the washing of water by the Word. Titus 3.5 says he saved us by the washing of regeneration and the renewal of the Holy Spirit. Of course, none of these verses specifically mention baptism, do they? But the terms washing and cleansing are, in my judgment, obvious references to the practice. That's disputed. Everything's disputed, right? Some downplay any reference to water baptism, saying that these verses refer only to the work of the Holy Spirit. I think that's a classic case of an either-or exegesis of a passage where a both-and solution fits better. The background to all these verses is the text I already quoted in the previous hour, Ezekiel 36, 25 through 27. Remember, there we have the idea of the cleansing with water and the gift of the Spirit. In 1 Corinthians 6.11, so I'm going to look at each of these verses briefly. In 1 Corinthians 3.11, there are three verbs that are used to describe our conversion. Washed, sanctified, and justified. Now, they all describe in this context what happens when we're converted. Yeah, even the verb sanctified, right? You're a very well-taught congregation. I know that. The word sanctified can refer to positional definitive sanctification that takes place at conversion. Even the order here supports that, right? Washed, sanctified, and then justified. Because of his progressive sanctification, Dr. Wallen would give Paul a D in terms of his systematic theology, right? Right, never, never. But Paul doesn't mean progressive sanctification here, right? He's using three terms to describe what happened when we become a Christian. You're cleansed, right? You're cleansed, that's symbolized and baptized. You're put in the realm of the holy. You're sanctified and you're justified. You're declared to be in the right by the divine judge. Three different terms all describing what it means to be saved. My point is, right, baptism is at the inception of the Christian life and symbolizes what? our washing away of sins, right? The cleansing of our sins. In Ephesians 5.26, Paul teaches when we're baptized, we are cleansed by the washing of the water. Paul, and then he says by means of the word. What does he mean by the word there? There's a number of views, but I think he has in mind there the word is the word of the gospel. it could be the profession of faith of the person but i think he has the gospel in mind there in titus 3 5 we read about the washing oh good i got a double blessing yeah great no that's great never be sorry about a double blessing but it isn't a second experience of grace right so It isn't that. So we have the washing of regeneration and the renewal of the Holy Spirit in Titus 3.5. And the words regeneration and renewal, I think both modify washing. And so regeneration and renewal are two ways of talking about, I think, the same thing. The washing constitutes regeneration and renewal. It doesn't follow that the washing is understood mechanically as if the water magically transforms people. It's not baptismal regeneration. Because he says both the regeneration and renewal here come from the Holy Spirit. The Spirit is given before, this is important isn't it, before one is washed in water. It is natural though that the readers would associate washing with their baptism since the gift of the Spirit and washing and baptism take place at the same time. What's the same time? I don't mean the same exact moment, but when one's converted, when one is saved. As I've said several times, or maybe just once, Spirit baptism and water baptism can be distinguished, but Paul often merges them together as he thinks about when people are saved. Right, people today, well, maybe not so much today. Maybe that was yesteryear now, but, you know, in previous Baptist life and evangelical life, people would say, I got saved when I went forward. But virtually none of those people meant that this going forward actually saved them, but it was associated with the time when they're converted. Well, that's actually how baptism functions in the New Testament, right? It's the time when you're saved and that washing symbolizes that salvation. Paul doesn't explicitly consider in these texts on washing whether baptism is fitting for infants. But when we think carefully about Titus 3, 5 through 7, we see that it rules out baptism for infants because we have seen that baptism is closely associated with the work of the Spirit in regenerating and renewing sinners so that they have new life. But this can't be true of infants unless we accept baptismal regeneration, which is a sacramental view. But that sacramental view must be rejected, since Paul also teaches justification in verse 6 of the very same chapter, right? And we know justification is by faith.",
